# Who must approve a change before it can merge.
#
# A MERGE gate, not a write permission. Anyone may propose a change to any path;
# this file decides whose approval the proposal needs — once "Require review
# from Code Owners" is on in the branch rule for `main`. Without that rule this
# file only suggests reviewers and enforces nothing.
#
# ── THE ONE MECHANICAL RULE ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# GitHub applies the LAST matching pattern, not the most specific one. So the
# broad tiers come first and the guardrails come last: a later narrow line
# overrides an earlier broad one. Reversing two blocks silently widens who can
# approve the most sensitive files, and nothing reports it. If you edit this
# file, keep the order.
#
# ── THE TIERS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Tier 2 — unowned. The README and other prose. CI still gates it; no approver.
#
#          Product code is NOT here, and that is the point. A product's files
#          are the *output* of a wish that a worker built, not a place outside
#          contributions land — the open-source project is the platform, and the
#          products are its demos. A pull request against a product folder from
#          someone who is not the worker is doing by hand what the wish pipeline
#          exists to do, and skips everything that pipeline enforces: the scope
#          guardrail, the approval rule, the critics, the receipt. So product
#          paths are owned, and CONTRIBUTING.md says to file a wish instead.
#
# Tier 1 — capability. The shared platform, the API, the tooling: code that is
#          load-bearing but whose failure mode is a bug. This is the tier to
#          delegate first. To hand it to more people, create a team and replace
#          @gavinxyang on the Tier 1 lines only:
#              /app/_platform/  @GTMinions/maintainers
#          Do not name a team that does not exist yet — GitHub treats an
#          unresolvable owner as no owner, so the path silently becomes
#          approvable by anyone. That fails open, which is the wrong direction.
#
# Tier 0 — principle. The guardrails, the trust boundary, the licence. A change
#          here can switch off the checks, move who may read what, or alter the
#          terms — so it is reviewed by the person accountable for it and is not
#          delegated as the project grows. This tier should almost never change;
#          if it changes often, something below it is in the wrong tier.

# ═══ TIER 1 — capability (delegate this tier first) ════════════════════════

/app/_platform/           @gavinxyang
/app/lib/                 @gavinxyang
/app/api/                 @gavinxyang
/scripts/                 @gavinxyang
# A deleted assertion leaves CI green. Weakening a test is a quiet way to
# weaken everything the test was standing in for.
/test/                    @gavinxyang
/vitest.config.ts         @gavinxyang
/CONTRIBUTING.md          @gavinxyang
# Product code arrives through the wish pipeline, authored by the worker, on a
# `wish/<n>` branch. Owning it here does not block that — the ruleset's bypass
# list covers the maintainer — it means a hand-written pull request against
# someone's generated demo gets read before it lands.
/app/(products)/          @gavinxyang

# ═══ TIER 0 — principle (never delegated; MUST stay below Tier 1) ══════════

# The checks themselves. Self-referential: a diff that disables the review is
# reviewed by what it disables, so it never leaves this tier.
